Topic A09

Traceable Retrieval Across Public Document Sources 

What problem are you investigating? 

When you're pulling documents from a number of public sources and processing them so they can be searched, every search result should be traceable back to the exact document it came from. That link is very easily lost. I'm looking at how to maintain the link between a search result, the original document version and the version of the processing that created it even in the case where the documents change and where a processing step fails and has to be re-run.
Here's an illustrative example (purely hypothetical, I've not seen this in practice). A pipeline (an automated sequence of steps) pulls documents off two public websites. One of the sites publishes agency policy notices and the other publishes technical reports. Each night the pipeline will download the new documents, extract the text, clean it up and add it to the search index. In the normal case a search result will show you the source site, the document and the version of the document that the text came from so a member of staff can go and open the original.
Two things can go wrong. Firstly, a source can edit a notice after it's been picked up and when the pipeline next comes along the new text will overwrite the old. A result built from the old text may now be pointing to a version which no longer exists and there's no way of knowing which version it's actually referring to. Secondly, a nightly run can be interrupted after 60 of 100 documents. If it then restarts from the beginning it could index those 60 documents twice and therefore produce duplicate results. If it restarts from a bad guess it could skip some of the documents and those would go missing. If the processing code has been updated in between then the results from one batch could be produced by different code versions and there would be no record of which version was used.
The boundaries are very narrow. The issue is about maintaining and retrieving the provenance that links a result back to its source and the processing that was applied. It's nothing to do with ranking quality, extracting text from a difficult format or anything to do with scale. It manifests itself when a source alters a document after it's been collected, when a document is collected more than once and when a step fails part way through and is subsequently re-run. It wouldn't be an issue in the case of a single collection of static documents with no failures at all.
Who or what is it going to affect?
Two groups are impacted: those who search the combined collection and those who need to check where a result originated from. The systems in question are the search index (which may contain results that no longer correspond to their source) and the pipeline (which will need to be fixed if a run fails).
The ramifications of the example are clear. Missing documents = missing information and the searcher has no idea what's missing. Duplicate documents = duplicate work as the reviewer will have to read the same document twice. A result based on a replaced version could lead to the wrong decision if someone treats it as though it's the current document. A staff member who opens the live source, sees different text and has no idea which is correct cannot validate the result. If you don't know what the processing version is then you can't explain any differences between results from one source.
A survey of data provenance from Indiana University goes some way to explaining why this is so important. The main uses of provenance cited are audit trails and data quality (sections 3.1.1 and 3.1.2) with the latter including the ability to trace errors in derived data back to the source data or process. With respect to data from the public domain (i.e. not from within a single organisation) the survey notes that you can't just assume the data is of good quality or veracious. You need a detailed history of the data to be able to assess its veracity and quality (section 2.1.1).
Source: Simmhan, Plale and Gannon, A Survey of Data Provenance Techniques, https://hdl.handle.net/2022/34466
