Attachment A (the list of problem sets) is clearly interested in related capabilities. Section 1.2 is all about the integration of multiple data sets and data formats, the ability to handle dynamic data flows and a semi-automated ETL (extract, transform and load) process where the data collected is selected, tagged and transformed for analysis. Section 1.8 is asking for answers that are derived from non-duplicated, deconflicted sources with accurate sourcing and section 1.9 is specifically about cross checking information from different sources. That said the document is very much in the AI for law enforcement and intelligence work camp and doesn't use the terms provenance, traceability, retry or describe the situation where processing has been interrupted. It does establish a need for multi-source integration and accurate sourcing and that's about it.
Source: https://biz.fbi.gov/broad-agency-announcement-baa/fbi-enterprise-baa-problem-sets
The IU survey defines provenance as the information which enables one to trace the derivation history of a data product back to its original sources (section 1). It states that a system needs to determine whether provenance is immutable, versioned when the data changes, or updated (section 3.4); that provenance records can include workflow versions (section 3.3.2); and that manually added provenance is often incomplete (section 3.4.2).
The Airflow documentation refers to a workflow as a DAG (a directed acyclic graph) consisting of a series of tasks, with the state of the tasks stored in a metadata database. With a versioned storage backend you can pin a specific version of the workflow code to each task, the default local disk setup not supporting versioning at all. One page states that only failed tasks can be rerun. The pages I looked at contained nothing at all on versioning of the source documents or on how a rerun would deal with partially written output.
Sources: https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/core-concepts/overview.html https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/index.html
Observed. I haven't made any observations or measurements and haven't run a pipeline or an experiment.
Assumptions and missing evidence. I'm assuming (with no evidence to date) that public sources do change documents frequently enough to be significant and that a naive retry will either duplicate or drop output. The latter follows from my example (not from any actual measurement). I have no evidence at all as to how common this is in real systems.
What solutions already exist?
I'm comparing scheduled batch processing with per-document pipelines. Airflow can orchestrate either of those.
Scheduled batch. A group of documents are collected and processed together at set times. The Airflow docs describe it as being ideal for batch workflows with a clear start and end that run on a schedule. Failed tasks can be re-run and past runs can be back-filled. A given run has a clear boundary so you can record what sources were collected, when and with what code version. The downside is that the run level record is very coarse. As the survey notes (section 3.2.2) provenance can be recorded at various levels of granularity and a batch record on its own doesn't tell you which version of which document produced the result. If a failed batch is re-run as a whole then documents could be reprocessed (depending on how the tasks are written) which the Airflow documentation I've looked at doesn't say.
Per document pipeline. Each document is processed as it comes in. The Airflow page covers both event triggered and time based workflows. Each document gets its own record which contains the source, version and step. A failure will be to one document so a retry is smaller. The survey cautions that provenance cost increases as you go from a high level of detail to a low level of detail and that very detailed records can end up being larger than the data itself (sections 3.2.2 and 3.4.1). That should be fine at my scale but there are going to be a lot more records to keep in sync.
Supporting pieces. Airflow's version pinning covers the processing version but not the source document versions. With regard to source history the survey compares the approach of storing the history with the data with the approach of re-computing it later by working backwards from the output and notes that the re-computation approach requires the source data to still be there so changing sources would require a lot of extra data to be retained (section 5). My interpretation (not the survey's actual statement) is that changing documents would require history to be recorded at the time of collection.
Neither of those approaches specifies what information must be saved. That's the gap that my project is intended to fill.
Sources: https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/core-concepts/overview.html, https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/index.html and https://hdl.handle.net/2022/34466
What's the one thing you still don't know?
The biggest unknown is what information we need to persist in order to be able to trace every search result back to the original source document version and the processing version after a failure and retry (with nothing lost and nothing duplicated). That in turn determines the design: if you persist too little you can't trace the results, if you persist too much the system becomes prohibitively expensive.
I'd deal with it via a small experiment using docs from two public sources and one search task. Get the docs, process them, produce a modified version of at least one (deliberately cutting the transformation step halfway through), re-run it and then re-run the search task. Then go through every result and check that it identifies its source, document version and processing version and that the number of docs missing and/or duplicated is as expected against the expected set.

I'd compare three record designs under the same test: (a) source address only, (b) source address plus a document version ID (e.g. collection time or a content fingerprint which is a short value derived from the text and changes when the text changes), and (c) (b) plus the processing version and a per-document completion flag. If the simplest design gives us zero missing/repeated results I'd go with that. If even the most complete design fails (e.g. if a document changes during a retry) then I'd extend it or look at per-document processing again.
My Individual Scope
I'll look at whether the info recorded for each document maintains traceability through one altered document and one interrupted/retried step. I'll exclude search quality and ranking, a UI, AI features, large scale operation, other failure types (network/hardware etc), and documents containing sensitive personal data. The main comparison will be between the three record designs above in a batch workflow, with per document processing carried out if time permits.
I'm going to use Python scripts, Airflow in standalone local mode with a SQLite database and a simple local search index. The Airflow install docs describe standalone mode as being for dev/test only and not for production so that's exactly what I'm after for a one person experiment. I'll pick two public sources which have very little personal information on them having checked the terms of use of each site.
Source: https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/howto/index.html
A possible (unproven) solution path would be to save for each document its source, version number, collection time, processing version and a completion flag so that on a retry you can skip the documents that have already been processed and the result can be checked.
The job is manageable for one student: one machine, two sources, one search task, one type of failure and no UI.
